                "Corporate globalization, cross-border mergers and acquisitions, and international supply chain integration require Indian domestic enterprises to deploy capital abroad into foreign subsidiaries, joint ventures, and international capital markets. To modernize and unify cross-border outbound investments, the Ministry of Finance and the Reserve Bank of India notified the Foreign Exchange Management (Overseas Investment) Rules, 2022, the Foreign Exchange Management (Overseas Investment) Regulations, 2022, and the Foreign Exchange Management (Overseas Investment) Directions, 2022. This streamlined framework replaced the legacy ODI regulations, formally bifurcating outward corporate capital flows into Overseas Direct Investment (ODI) and Overseas Portfolio Investment (OPI), establishing clear quantitative financial commitment ceilings, and instituting strict regulatory boundaries against round-tripping and aggressive tax structuring.",
                "Under the 2022 regime, Overseas Direct Investment (ODI) is defined as investment by way of acquisition of unlisted equity capital of a foreign entity, or subscription to the memorandum of association of a foreign entity, or investment in 10 percent or more of the paid-up equity capital of a listed foreign entity, or investment with control in a listed foreign entity. Control is defined as the right to appoint that majority of the directors or to control the management or policy decisions exercisable by a person or persons acting individually or in concert. Conversely, Overseas Portfolio Investment (OPI) encompasses investments in listed foreign securities other than ODI, non-controlling equity participation of less than 10 percent in listed foreign companies, overseas debt securities, and foreign mutual fund units. For Indian Entities (encompassing companies incorporated under the Companies Act, registered partnership firms, Limited Liability Partnerships, and body corporates created under Acts of Parliament), the statutory financial commitment ceiling under the Automatic Route is established at 400 percent of the Indian Entity's audited Net Worth as per the last audited balance sheet.",
