                "External Commercial Borrowings (ECBs) constitute commercial loan instruments raised by eligible resident Indian borrowers from recognized non-resident lenders, serving as a critical channel for mobilizing foreign capital to finance domestic capital expenditure, infrastructure projects, and balance sheet expansion. Regulated by the Reserve Bank of India under the Master Direction on External Commercial Borrowings, Trade Credits and Structured Obligations, the ECB regulatory framework governs foreign currency denominated ECBs (such as US Dollar, Euro, or Japanese Yen loans) and Indian Rupee (INR) denominated ECBs, establishing precise parameters for eligible borrowers, recognized lenders, minimum average maturity periods, all-in-cost ceilings, and permissible end-uses.",
                "The ECB framework operates through two distinct regulatory channels: the Automatic Route and the Approval Route. Under the Automatic Route, eligible borrowers—encompassing all entities eligible to receive Foreign Direct Investment (FDI), port trusts, units in Special Economic Zones (SEZs), and Small Industries Development Bank of India (SIDBI)—may raise ECBs up to a statutory ceiling of USD 750 million (or its equivalent in other convertible currencies) per financial year without requiring prior RBI sanction, subject to strict compliance with prudential parameters. Borrowing proposals exceeding USD 750 million, or proposals falling outside the standard parameters of maturity or pricing, require prior regulatory vetting and formal sanction from the Reserve Bank's Empowered Committee under the Approval Route.",
